<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    =========================================================================
    DnnMigration.Api : the API layer of the DnnMigration Clean Architecture
    solution (AAP 0.4.1.1 / 0.4.3).

    This is the ASP.NET Core 8 Backend for Frontend host that replaces the
    legacy Web Forms request pipeline. Website/Default.aspx.vb (700 lines,
    InitializePage L90, LoadSkin L217, ManageRequest L304, ManageStyleSheets
    L355, Page_Init L499, Page_Load L639, Page_PreRender L648) and the
    Website/release.config module chain (8 HTTP modules at L67 to L74, 7
    handler entries at L77 to L83) are NOT ported: all of it collapses into
    the single explicit middleware pipeline declared in Program.cs.

    This is the ONLY project in the solution built with Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web.
    Domain, Application and Infrastructure all use plain Microsoft.NET.Sdk.

    Rule T1, layer inward only: this project references Application and
    Infrastructure, and nothing else, so a layering violation is a compile
    error rather than a review finding. Rule T2: controllers validate the
    request shape, delegate to an Application service and translate the
    result into an HTTP status code. They hold no business logic.
    =========================================================================
  -->

  <!--
    #########################################################################
    #  CONTAINER ENTRYPOINT CONTRACT : NO COMPILER WILL CATCH A MISMATCH.   #
    #  Do NOT add an assembly name or root namespace property to this file. #
    #########################################################################

    docker/api.Dockerfile publishes this project framework dependent and ends
    with:

        ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "DnnMigration.Api.dll"]

    The SDK derives both the assembly name and the root namespace from this
    project's FILE NAME, so DnnMigration.Api.csproj yields the assembly
    DnnMigration.Api and therefore exactly the DnnMigration.Api.dll that the
    container starts. Declaring either value explicitly creates a second
    place from which the name can silently drift; AAP 0.1.2.3 is explicit
    that resolving it incorrectly "breaks the container build in a way no
    compiler will catch".

    The file name is therefore a hard cross folder contract in three places:
    backend/DnnMigration.sln enumerates
    src\DnnMigration.Api\DnnMigration.Api.csproj, the integration test
    project references ..\..\src\DnnMigration.Api\DnnMigration.Api.csproj,
    and api.Dockerfile copies and restores that same path before publishing.
    The build stage runs on mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0-alpine, whose
    filesystem is case sensitive, so the casing must stay exactly as it is.
    (The Angular workspace deliberately uses the lower case hyphenated name
    dnn-migration; the two conventions must never be interchanged.)
  -->

  <!--
    Build policy is INHERITED from backend/Directory.Build.props, which
    MSBuild imports implicitly by walking upward from this directory. The
    .NET 8 target framework, language version 12, nullable reference types,
    implicit usings, warnings as errors, the bounded warning suppression
    list, XML documentation generation and code style enforcement are all
    declared there, once. backend/global.json pins the SDK to 8.0.423 and
    backend/.editorconfig keeps every style rule advisory so that code style
    enforcement cannot break the build.

    That is why this project file declares no property group at all: one
    decision, one place to change it. The suppression list is bounded to
    exactly CS8618 and CS1591 solution wide (AAP 0.6.3, "no other warning is
    suppressed"). If this project ever emits any other diagnostic under
    `dotnet build -configuration Release -warnaserror`, FIX THE CODE: never
    add a suppression, an exemption or an analyser level override here.

    Container hardening (AAP 0.8.3): api.Dockerfile creates and switches to
    the unprivileged user `adduser -D -u 1000 appuser` and listens on 8080,
    so nothing here may require root, a privileged port below 1024, or a
    writable path outside the application directory. Secrets reach the host
    as environment variables (ConnectionStrings__Default, Jwt__Secret), never
    from a project scoped secret store.
  -->

  <!--
    Package ownership follows the layering (AAP 0.6.1): the API layer owns
    the JWT bearer, OpenAPI/Swagger, API versioning and Serilog references,
    and only those. Every version is an exact pin, with no wildcard, no
    floating range and no moving tag anywhere (AAP 0.8.3), and the 8.0.29
    band matches the installed Microsoft.AspNetCore.App and
    Microsoft.NETCore.App shared frameworks exactly.

    DELIBERATELY ABSENT because Infrastructure owns them and they arrive
    transitively through the project reference below: EF Core with its
    relational, SQL Server and design time companions (Rule T3 keeps the
    database context internal to Infrastructure), the SQL Server data client
    (pinned there at 5.2.3 to defeat a transitive downgrade), the password
    hashing library and the SQL Server health check package. The validation
    library is likewise Application owned and transitive.

    ALSO DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the Serilog console sink. It appears in the
    superseded prior specification at docs/technical-specifications.md L937
    but NOT in the AAP 0.6.1 dependency set, and Serilog.AspNetCore already
    carries what the request logging middleware needs. The object to object
    mapping library is rejected solution wide in favour of hand written
    static mappers (AAP 0.4.3). Rate limiting, CORS and health check
    endpoint mapping all ship in the ASP.NET Core 8 shared framework that the
    Web SDK brings in implicitly, so none of them needs a package here.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Swashbuckle.AspNetCore" Version="6.9.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Asp.Versioning.Mvc" Version="8.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Asp.Versioning.Mvc.ApiExplorer" Version="8.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" Version="8.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly two project references (AAP 0.5.2.2). Application supplies the
    service abstractions, DTOs and options classes the controllers bind to,
    plus the AddApplication() registration extension. Infrastructure is
    referenced for one reason only: Program.cs calls
    AddInfrastructure(builder.Configuration) as the composition root, which
    registers the database context, the nine repositories, the unit of work,
    the security services and the database health check that
    MapHealthChecks("/health") surfaces. That endpoint must stay anonymous,
    because the frontend container waits on the API health check.

    The Domain project is NOT referenced explicitly. It arrives transitively
    through both references, and naming it here would advertise that the API
    may consume domain entities directly, contradicting the DTO boundary rule
    that no entity crosses the wire in either direction (AAP 0.8.3).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Infrastructure\DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
